# Architecture for ArchiPy Apps

Use this when building applications that depend on the `archipy` package (not when maintaining ArchiPy itself).

## Layer Map

```text
your_app/
├── models/       # Domain — data structures only (entities, DTOs, errors, types)
├── adapters/     # Infrastructure — external systems (or wrappers around ArchiPy adapters)
├── helpers/      # Support — utils, decorators, interceptors (pure; no DB/I/O)
├── configs/      # Configuration — BaseConfig subclasses, DI containers
├── repositories/ # Orchestrate adapters; map to DTOs
├── logics/       # Use-case / unit-of-work boundaries
└── services/     # Transport (HTTP/gRPC routers), versioned
```

## Layer Rules

### `models/`
- Data structures only — no business logic, no I/O.
- Prefer ArchiPy base entities/errors where applicable; subclass domain errors from ArchiPy `BaseError` hierarchy.
- DTOs: Pydantic `BaseModel`. Version public domain DTOs under `domain/v{n}/`.

### `adapters/`
- Ports & adapters: abstract port + concrete adapter (+ optional mock for BDD).
- Prefer ArchiPy adapters via extras (`uv add "archipy[redis]"`) before inventing clients.
- Catch infrastructure exceptions at the boundary and re-raise as domain errors with `raise ... from e`.

### `helpers/`
- Pure utilities only — no database calls, no network I/O, no adapter construction.
- Sub-packages: `utils/`, `decorators/`, `interceptors/`.
- Prefer ArchiPy helpers before writing custom ones.
- Decorators and interceptors must not import concrete adapter implementations at module level.

### `configs/`
- Extend `archipy.configs.base_config.BaseConfig`.
- Call `BaseConfig.set_global(config)` once at bootstrap.
- No hardcoded secrets — document required vars in `.env.example`.

## Import Direction (one-way)

```text
configs  ←  models  ←  helpers  ←  adapters / repositories / logics / services
```

- Never import upward (e.g. models must not import adapters or logics).
- Services/logics may depend on ports (abstractions), not concrete adapter modules when DI is used.

## Python Conventions (apps)

- Python 3.14+: `X | Y`, `list[str]`, `dict[str, int]`.
- Double quotes for strings.
- Google-style docstrings on public functions/classes.
- Specific exceptions; always `raise ... from e`.
